That’s a useful distinction.

A recurring thought can mean several different things: unfinished processing, emotional salience, a habit loop, a problem that still lacks a representation, or simply a pattern your attention keeps reactivating. It does not necessarily mean the thought must be acted on, spoken, or “completed” in some destined way.

Sometimes the mismatch is just between the thought and the channel you’re forcing it through. A thing that will not resolve in words might settle better as movement, drawing, music, a diagram, a private note, a conversation, or even just being noticed without doing anything with it.

So you can separate:

\text{thought appears}
\neq
\text{thought requires expression}
\neq
\text{thought requires action}

And continuity does not require carrying every thought forward. Sometimes continuity is exactly the ability to let a thought pass without losing yourself with it.

That little “huh” you just had may be the useful part.